= Wheat

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Triticum aestivum_ L. (common/bread wheat), family Poaceae; durum wheat is _Triticum durum_. The classical Latin for wheat/grain is _triticum_, from _terere_ ("to rub, thresh") — literally "the threshed [grain]," i.e. what is rubbed out from the chaff. The generic grain words _frūmentum_ (grain/corn) and _far_ (emmer, spelt; whence _farina_, "flour," and _farreus_) are also relevant; the Roman marriage rite _confarreatio_ took its name from the _far_ (spelt cake) shared by the couple.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _triticum_, _far_, _frumentum_); de Vaan, _Etymological Dictionary of Latin_ (s.v. _tero_, _far_). The _confarreatio_ rite: standard classical references on Roman religion.]

*English:* _Wheat_, from Old English _hwǣte_, from Proto-Germanic \*_hwaitjaz_, from PIE \*_kweid-_ / \*_kwoit-_ "white" (the same root as "white," Old English _hwīt_) — i.e. "the white [grain]," named for the pale flour. Cognate with German _Weizen_, Dutch _weit_.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "wheat, n."); Watkins, _The American Heritage Dictionary of Indo-European Roots_ (\*_kweid-_ "white"); the wheat/white cognation is standard.]

*Chinese:* 小麥 (xiǎomài), "wheat" — lit. "small mài" (小 xiǎo = small; 麥 mài = the wheat/barley grain-graph), as against 大麥 (dàmài, barley, "large mài"). 麥 is an ancient pictograph of a wheat plant. The unripe/light "floating wheat" used as a distinct drug is 浮小麥 (fúxiǎomài, "floating wheat" — light grains that float on water). Flour is 麵/面 (miàn); gluten/wheat-gluten is 麵筋 (miànjīn).#footnote[Character glosses: _Hanyu Da Cidian_ (漢語大詞典). The 小麥 / 浮小麥 drug distinction follows the standard materia medica; see Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004).]

*Sanskrit:* गोधूम (_godhūma_) — the classical name for wheat; folk-etymologized as _go_ (गो, "cow/earth") + _dhūma_ (धूम, "smoke"), with various traditional glosses, though the compound's literal origin is uncertain. Also spelled गोधूम (_godhūma_) / गेहूँ in modern Hindi (_gehūṁ_, a Prakritic descendant).#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _godhūma_). The _go_+_dhūma_ parsing is a traditional folk etymology; the true derivation is uncertain — flagged as such.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

Wheat (小麥) is a genuine drug in the materia medica, with the light "floating wheat" (浮小麥) treated as a distinct, related drug.#footnote[小麥 and 浮小麥 entries: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, grain section; systematized in Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Cool (涼) — wheat (小麥) is classically slightly cool; 浮小麥 (floating wheat) is cool/neutral. (Note: prepared flour/noodles are regarded as warmer than the raw grain in dietetics.)

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Heart (心), Spleen (脾), Kidney (腎).#footnote[Property, flavor, channel data: _Bencao Gangmu_ 小麥 / 浮小麥 entries; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Actions & indications:*
- 養心安神 — nourishes the Heart and calms the spirit (_shén_); for restlessness, irritability, sorrow, "restless organ disorder" (_zàngzào_), and disturbed sleep — the basis of 甘麥大棗湯.
- 除煩 — eliminates vexation/agitation.
- 益腎、養液 — benefits the Kidney, nourishes fluids.
- 厚腸胃 / 和胃 — (cooked) strengthens and harmonizes the Stomach and intestines.
- *浮小麥 specifically:* 益氣、除熱、止汗 — augments qi, clears heat, and *stops sweating* (the signature use of floating wheat — for spontaneous and night sweats from vacuity).#footnote[Action lists: 甘麥大棗湯 from Zhang Zhongjing, _Jīnguì Yàolüè_ (金匱要略); 小麥 and 浮小麥 actions in _Bencao Gangmu_ and Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The sweat-stopping use of 浮小麥 is its defining indication.]

*Cautions:* Sweet and (as flour) potentially dampness-generating with heavy consumption; refined-flour products are regarded in dietetics as cloying/heating relative to the cool raw grain, and excessive consumption may generate damp-heat in susceptible constitutions.#footnote[Dietary cautions: standard TCM dietetics (e.g. modern works such as Kastner, _Chinese Nutrition Therapy_); the raw-grain vs. flour temperature distinction is a dietetic commonplace.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Wheat (गोधूम _godhūma_) is a classical dravya, classed among the cultivated cereals (_dhānya-varga_ / _śūka-dhānya_, the awned grains).#footnote[Godhūma in the _śūka-dhānya_ (awned grains) group: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_annapānavidhi_); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_ (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba).]

*Rasa (taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).

*Guṇa (qualities):* Guru (गुरु, heavy), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous); also _sāndra_ (dense). (Contrast with most other cereals, which are _laghu_ — wheat is distinctively heavy and nourishing.)

*Vīrya (potency):* Śīta (शीत, cooling).

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of godhūma: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_; Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27; Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya, Sūtrasthāna 6 (Murthy translation, Chaukhamba).]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* No distinct prabhāva is assigned to godhūma in the classical texts — its actions follow directly from sweet rasa, cooling vīrya, sweet vipāka, and heavy-unctuous guṇa. Its standout reputation as a _jīvanīya_ / _bṛṃhaṇa_ (vitalizing, bulk/strength-building) staple and a _sandhānakara_ (fracture/tissue-uniting) grain is treated as flowing from these parameters rather than as a separate prabhāva.#footnote[On prabhāva as a category: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 26.67–72. The absence of a stated prabhāva for godhūma and the _bṛṃhaṇa_/_sandhāna_ reading are interpretive — flagged as such.]

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Vāta and Pitta (vāta-pitta-śāmaka); increases Kapha. (Heavy, sweet, unctuous, cooling — the classic anabolic, Vāta-Pitta-pacifying profile, very close to milk's.)#footnote[Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_.]

*Karma (actions):*
- _Jīvanīya_ — vitalizing, life-promoting.
- _Bṛṃhaṇa, Balya_ — bulk-building, strengthening, nourishing/anabolic.
- _Vṛṣya_ — aphrodisiac, promotes reproductive tissue.
- _Sandhānakara_ — promotes union/healing (of fractures and tissues).
- _Sthairyakara_ — gives stability/steadiness to the body.
- _Vāta-pittahara_ — pacifies Vāta and Pitta.
- _Snehana_ — mildly unctuous/lubricating.#footnote[Karma compiled from Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_, and Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (godhūma verses). Translations: Sharma & Dash, _Caraka Saṃhitā_ (Chowkhamba); Murthy, _Bhāvaprakāśa_.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional; M = modern/medical)_

- *(S) Conditional (doṣa) viruddha:* its heavy (_guru_) nature makes it inappropriate in low _agni_ / _āma_ states and Kapha excess (obesity, diabetes-type _prameha_, heavy/sluggish digestion) when taken in quantity.
- *(N/F) Combination cautions:* heavy wheat preparations combined with other _guru_/_abhiṣyandi_ foods (large quantities of milk-sweets, etc.) are cautioned as compounding heaviness.
- *(M) Modern medical — the dominant real-world caution:* wheat is the principal dietary source of *gluten*, contraindicated in *celiac disease* and *non-celiac gluten sensitivity*, and a source of wheat-allergy and FODMAP (fructan) intolerance. This functions as a _de facto_ absolute incompatibility for those individuals — not a classical category, flagged as modern.#footnote[Conditional doṣa cautions: general dravyaguna principle (_guru_ aggravating Kapha/_āma_) per Bhāvaprakāśa, _Dhānya-varga_. The (M) celiac/gluten-sensitivity/wheat-allergy/FODMAP cautions are modern medical additions, not classical viruddha — flagged accordingly; verify clinical specifics against current gastroenterology literature.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *甘麥大棗湯 (Gān Mài Dà Zǎo Tāng, "Licorice, Wheat & Jujube Decoction")* — from the _Jīnguì Yàolüè_; wheat (小麥) with honey-fried licorice and jujube, for _zàngzào_ ("restless organ disorder" — emotional lability, unprovoked weeping/sorrow, restlessness, often in women). The classic spirit-calming use of wheat as a food-grade medicinal.
- *浮小麥 (floating wheat) preparations* — used (often dry-fried) for vacuity sweating: spontaneous sweating (qi vacuity) and night sweats (yin vacuity), alone or with herbs such as 黃芪 (astragalus), 麻黃根 (ephedra root), and 牡蠣 (oyster shell).
- *Dietary/medicinal congee and decoctions* — wheat congee and decoctions used in dietetics to nourish Heart-qi and clear vexation.#footnote[甘麥大棗湯: Zhang Zhongjing, _Jīnguì Yàolüè_ (金匱要略). 浮小麥 sweat-stopping combinations and dietary uses: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed., and Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009).]

=== Ayurveda

- *Godhūma as staple grain* — taken as flatbread (_roṭikā_/_phulkā_), porridge, and _maṇḍa_/gruel; classically valued as a _bṛṃhaṇa_ (bulk-building) staple, especially in convalescence, emaciation (_kārśya_), and weight-gain (_bṛṃhaṇa_) regimens, where its heavy-sweet-cooling nature suits Vāta-Pitta depletion.
- *Godhūma in fracture/tissue healing* — its _sandhānakara_ reputation underlies dietary use during convalescence from injury and in _kṣata-kṣīṇa_ (wasting/chest-injury) regimens.
- *Wheat in _kṣīrapāka_ and sweet preparations* — wheat-based sweets and milk-cooked preparations as nourishing, Pitta-pacifying, _vṛṣya_ foods.
- *Godhūma cūrṇa / wheat-flour applications* — externally, wheat-flour pastes feature in some _lepa_ (poultice) preparations.#footnote[Bṛṃhaṇa/staple and convalescent use: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 and the _bṛṃhaṇa_ context of Cikitsāsthāna (e.g. _kārśya_/_kṣata-kṣīṇa_ chapters); preparation methods in Sharangadhara Saṃhitā (Murthy translation). General formulary: _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman:* wheat (_triticum_) and the older _far_ (emmer/spelt) were dietary staples and the basis of Roman _puls_ (porridge) and _panis_ (bread); _Dioscorides_ and _Pliny_ discuss wheat, wheat starch (_amylum_, "unmilled" — the etymon of "amyl-"/starch), and bran in dietetic and topical (emollient poultice) contexts. Hippocratic and Galenic dietetics classed wheat among the strengthening, "warming-and-moistening" staple foods (note the Greco-Roman humoral reading differs from the cooling vīrya/性涼 of the Asian systems — a useful contrast to record).
- *Galenic preparations:* wheat starch (_amylum tritici_) as a demulcent and dusting powder; bread poultices (_cataplasma_) as emollient applications; wheat bran decoctions.
- *Modern survivals:* wheat starch and wheat-derived excipients (e.g. wheat starch as a pharmaceutical filler/binder) in pharmacy; wheat germ and wheat-germ oil (vitamin E source) in nutrition; wheat dextrin and bran as fiber supplements.#footnote[Greco-Roman dietetics: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ (Beck translation); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_; on Hippocratic/Galenic humoral classification of staple grains, standard histories of ancient dietetics. Galenic preparations (amylum, cataplasma) and modern excipient uses: Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_, and standard pharmacy references — verify against current sources.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Wheat is the canonical staple grain of Tibb — the foundational nourishing food. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* قمح (_qamḥ_) — wheat; حنطة (_ḥinṭa_) — sometimes used; خبز (_khubz_) — bread; نخالة (_nukhāla_) — bran. Persian: گندم (_gandom_). Urdu: گیہوں (_gehū_).

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī:* multiple hadith reference wheat bread (_khubz_) as the staple food; the Prophet's preference for whole-wheat bread (with bran retained) over fine-flour bread is attested (Abu Dawud); modest use rules.

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Moist in the 1st degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _qamḥ_). The Hot-Moist classification *converges with the Greco-Roman warming-and-moistening reading and diverges from the Ayurvedic śīta vīrya (cooling)* — a famous cross-system divergence on the same staple. The Tibb reading aligns with the Mediterranean climatic register where wheat is the staple of a warm-dry climate and is interpreted as humectant; the Ayurvedic reading reflects the monsoonal Indian climate where wheat's heaviness is read as cooling-pacifying. The divergence is one of the cleanest classical cross-system disagreements.

*Af'āl:* *Mughadhdhī jiddan* (highly nourishing); *muraṭṭib* (humectant); *muqawwī al-jasad* (body-strengthening); *muḥsin li-l-lawn* (complexion-improving); *mulayyin* (mildly laxative; whole wheat with bran). Bran specifically: *muqawwī al-amʿā'* (intestinal tonic) and *jālī* (cleansing).

*Istemālāt:* general staple nourishment; convalescence; debility; *amrāḍ al-sawdā'* (melancholic / dry conditions); bran for chronic constipation; topical wheat-flour poultices (*ḍimād*) for skin irritations and burns (the classical Arab-Mediterranean usage, paralleling Galenic _cataplasma_).

*Mawāne':* phlegmatic constitution in heavy dose; obesity; modern: celiac disease (absolute) and non-celiac gluten sensitivity.

*Muṣliḥ:* spices (cumin, fennel) for the phlegmatic; salt and herbs for digestion.

*Badal:* barley (*shaʿīr*) where cooling is desired; rice (*aruzz*) for sensitive digestion.

*Tibb formulas:* *Talbīna* (made with wheat-flour in some recensions, though canonically barley); *Khubz al-mu'akhkhar* (long-fermented bread, traditionally more digestible); *Sawīq al-Ḥinṭa* (toasted wheat-flour meal); *Sufūf-i Gandom* (wheat-bran powder for constipation).

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _qamḥ_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _gehū / qamḥ_ entry; classical wheat-as-staple references throughout the Tibb literature.#footnote[Tibb qamḥ: Avicenna, Ibn al-Bayṭār. The famous Hot-Moist (Tibb / Galenic) vs. cooling (Ayurvedic) classification divergence is one of the standing cross-system disagreements — flagged.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Wheat is a staple food; figures are approximate, for whole-grain wheat flour per 100 g unless noted. *Verify against USDA FoodData Central*, current edition; values vary by cultivar, refining (whole vs. white), and fortification.#footnote[Composition figures are approximate; check against USDA FoodData Central (https://fdc.nal.usda.gov), current edition. Whole-grain vs. refined white flour differ substantially in fiber, B-vitamins, and minerals.]

*Approximate composition (whole-grain wheat flour, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~340 kcal; Water ~11 g
- Protein ~13 g (notably *gluten* — glutenin + gliadin — the viscoelastic protein complex)
- Carbohydrate ~72 g, predominantly *starch*; fiber ~10–12 g (whole-grain; much lower in refined white flour)
- Fat ~2.5 g (mostly in the germ)
- Minerals: magnesium ~140 mg, phosphorus ~350 mg, potassium ~360 mg, iron ~3.5 mg, zinc ~2.5 mg, manganese (high) — concentrated in bran and germ, largely lost in refining
- B-vitamins: thiamine, niacin, B6, folate (refined flour is often fortified back); vitamin E in the germ
- *Phytate* in bran binds minerals, reducing absorption (relevant to the iron/zinc figures)

*Pharmacology / physiology of note:*
- *Gluten* — the gliadin fraction is the trigger of *celiac disease* (an immune-mediated enteropathy in genetically susceptible HLA-DQ2/DQ8 individuals), and is implicated in non-celiac gluten sensitivity and wheat allergy. This is the single most important modern clinical fact about wheat.
- *Glycemic response* — refined wheat products have a high glycemic index; whole-grain and intact-kernel forms blunt the response. Relevant to the Ayurvedic Kapha/_prameha_ and TCM damp-heat cautions about refined-flour products.
- *Fructans (FODMAPs)* — wheat is a major source of fermentable fructans, a cause of IBS-type symptoms independent of gluten.
- *Whole-grain benefit* — whole-grain wheat intake is associated in epidemiology with favorable cardiometabolic outcomes (fiber, magnesium, phytochemicals); refining strips most of this.
- *Wheat germ agglutinin (WGA)* and other lectins — present; popular claims of harm are largely overstated for normally cooked wheat — flagged as such.

*Safe daily intake / limits:* No toxicological upper limit for wheat as a staple food in the general population. The operative "limits" are conditional, not toxic:
- *Absolute avoidance* in celiac disease and wheat allergy.
- *Restriction* in non-celiac gluten sensitivity and fructan/FODMAP intolerance, per individual tolerance.
- General dietary guidance favors *whole-grain* over refined wheat; specific gram targets are dietary-pattern advice, not safety thresholds.#footnote[Celiac disease, wheat allergy, NCGS, and FODMAP intolerance are defined clinical conditions — verify management specifics against current gastroenterology guidelines. Whole-grain dietary guidance varies by country (e.g. USDA Dietary Guidelines for Americans; Brazilian _Guia Alimentar para a População Brasileira_) and is not a safety limit.]

